    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. In reality, the escrow account is typically held by a neutral third-party service, such as a bank or a lawyer, who acts as an escrow agent. They hold the funds until the conditions of the sale are met, and then release them to the buyer.\n\nThink of it like a 'goblin's trap' – the escrow agent is like a clever little creature that holds the funds until the right moment, when the conditions are just right. When the conditions are met, the 'trap' is sprung, and the funds are released.\n\nIn Toronto, the escrow agent is usually a lawyer or a title company that holds the funds until the closing date. They ensure that the funds are released only when the seller has fulfilled their obligations, such as completing repairs or providing a clear title.\n\nSo, to answer your original question, an escrow account is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met, and it's used in the closing process to ensure that the transaction is smooth and hassle-free. No goblins involved, I promise!",
